---
title: SurfDoc Size-Class Axis
type: spec
status: active
created: "2026-08-24"
updated: "2026-08-24"
---

::summary
The 0.18 responsive axis. Three size classes, two breakpoints, resolved
ONCE in Rust and consumed unchanged by web, iOS and Android. Authors write
per-class values and class conditionals; no client carries its own
breakpoint table.
::

## The three classes

::data
| Class | Width range (logical pt / dp / css-px @1x) | Constant |
|---|---|---|
| `mobile` | `width < 768` | — |
| `tablet` | `768 <= width < 1024` | `SIZE_CLASS_TABLET_MIN = 768` |
| `desktop` | `width >= 1024` | `SIZE_CLASS_DESKTOP_MIN = 1024` |
::

Boundaries are half-open: 767 is still `mobile`, 768 is `tablet`, 1023 is
still `tablet`, 1024 is `desktop`. The resolver `resolve_size_class(width)`
is total — every `u32`, including 0 and `u32::MAX`, resolves. An
out-of-range or nonsense width can never break a render.

The two constants are the whole contract with clients. They cross the FFI
as `size_class_tablet_min()` and `size_class_desktop_min()` (UniFFI 0.28
cannot export a plain constant), and `assets/surfdoc.css` spells the same
four numbers — 767, 768, 1023, 1024 — in its chrome media queries.
`tests/css_coverage.rs` asserts the CSS numbers equal the Rust constants,
so the two can never drift apart.

::callout[type=warning title="Two breakpoint canons, not one"]
The content-block queries at 480, 640 and 720 are a SEPARATE, frozen
visual canon. The size-class axis does not touch them, and no chrome rule
may use them.
::

## Typed layouts

`::app-shell[layout=]` is a closed three-value set:

::data
| Value | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `sidebar-main-panel` | The classic three-column desktop shell (default) |
| `tabs` | Tab-bar-first at every class |
| `adaptive` | Per-class navigation, configured by sub-attrs |
::

`layout=adaptive` takes `mobile=` / `tablet=` / `desktop=` sub-attrs drawn
from `{tabs, rail, sidebar}`. Absent sub-attrs take the platform defaults:
tabs on mobile, rail on tablet, sidebar on desktop.

`::page[layout=]` is the recognized set `{default, hero, cards, split}`.

Unknown values in either vocabulary DEGRADE to the default and raise lint
**L041**. A stale or misspelled layout is a warning, never a failed render
— the same discipline as `resolve_style_pack`.

## Per-size-class values

An attribute that varies by class is authored as a whitespace-separated
list in mobile / tablet / desktop order:

::code[lang=surf]
::sidebar[position=left width="0 72 260"]
::features[cols="1 2 3"]
::tab-content[tab="main" width="0 700 880"]
::

Degradation ladder — parsing never fails:

- one token: broadcasts to all three classes, byte-identical to the
  pre-0.18 scalar attribute
- two tokens: mobile, tablet; desktop inherits tablet
- three or more: mobile, tablet, desktop (extras ignored)
- empty, or ANY non-numeric token: the attribute is absent, i.e. the
  block's default applies

Carriers today: `cols` on `::features` and `::product-grid`, `columns` on
`::gallery` (the attribute name differs and both names are frozen), and
`width` on `::sidebar`, `::drawer` and `::tab-content`.

A uniform value re-serializes as the bare scalar and serializes over the
wire as the bare scalar; only a genuinely varying triple widens. Every
pre-0.18 document therefore round-trips byte-identically.

## Class conditionals

::data
| Attribute | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `classes=` | Comma list of classes the block is shown in |
| `min-class=` | Smallest class the block appears in |
::

Both are accepted on `::sidebar`, `::panel`, `::tab-content` and
`::drawer`. Unknown tokens are dropped; an all-unknown list is treated as
absent (block shows everywhere).

`::panel[desktop-only=true]` is a DEPRECATED alias for `classes=desktop`.
It still parses, still normalizes into the same class set, and lint
**L042** offers a safe fix that rewrites it. The raw flag is preserved so
the re-serializer emits the source form it read and never both spellings.

## Resolving once

The render paths take no width and never have. The seam is the
projection:

::code[lang=rust]
let class = surf_parse::resolve_size_class(width);   // 390 -> Mobile
let doc = parsed.for_width(390);                     // or .for_size_class(class)
let html = doc.to_html();
::

`for_width` collapses every per-class value to that class's value and
drops the chrome blocks the class gates out. A document that uses none of
the 0.18 attributes comes back unchanged — pinned by
`projection_is_identity_for_documents_without_the_axis` in
`tests/corpus.rs`.

## What crosses the FFI

Native schema **v5** (`NATIVE_DOC_SCHEMA_VERSION`):

- `NativePerClassU32` — a resolved mobile/tablet/desktop triple, carried by
  `Sidebar.width`, `Drawer.width`, `TabContent.width`, `Gallery.columns`,
  `Features.cols` and `ProductGrid.cols`
- `NativeClassGate` on `Sidebar` / `Panel` / `TabContent` / `Drawer`
- `AppShell.adaptive` — the resolved navigation modes
- `NativeTabBarItem.unread` and `.role`, plus `TabContent.width` / `.align`
  — values that reached HTML but died at the FFI before v5
- `size_class_tablet_min()`, `size_class_desktop_min()`,
  `resolve_size_class(width)`

## HTML hooks

::data
| Attribute | Source |
|---|---|
| `data-size-class="mobile tablet"` | `classes=` |
| `data-min-size-class="tablet"` | `min-class=` |
| `data-size-class-width="0 700 880"` | a varying `width=` |
| `data-cols` / `data-cols-tablet` / `data-cols-desktop` | a varying `cols=` |
| `data-adaptive-mobile` / `-tablet` / `-desktop` | `layout=adaptive` |
::

For a varying width the renderer emits ONLY the three `--sc-w-*` custom
properties inline; the property itself is selected in `assets/surfdoc.css`
Section 80. An inline shorthand would out-specify the media queries and the
element would never resize.

When a block authors none of these attributes, no hook is emitted and the
markup is byte-identical to 0.17.
